  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "No, first of all, let me just say thank you for everyone being here because we are in a critical time, not only in our community, but across this amazing America that we live in. And so I think when you ask us, that's the question that we get a lot. What are we doing in Congress? Well, first of all, we have to be able to communicate and let people know what's really happening. And I think we're doing a great job at that. Just recently I held a tele town hall and did it in a studio with other members of Congress so we could get out the information. We had some 24,000 people on social media to stream it. We had 8,400 people who stayed on for most of the tele town hall where we walked them through what that chaos and confusion. Is that enough? Absolutely not. We're doing legacy media or traditional media, but we're also going to what I call new school media. So many people are on social media. They are looking at Instagram. They're looking at X and Twitter and TikTok if you must. And so those are the things that we also have to start communicating with. And we also have to do podcasts. So nothing's off-limit right now. But I do think is important. And this is very personal to me. When you start talking about slashing health care, When you start talking about taking away funds to the tune of 800 billions of dollars, when we talk about food service, we talk about our children, when you talk about dismantling our public educational system, it becomes very personal. I'm a product of public schools and public and state supported universities. And I'm very proud of that. And I think if you look at my track record, I did OK in those public schools. So we are also out in the fight. I believe I'm unapologetic about who I am. I believe in standing up and fighting when they were attacking our voting rights. I got arrested for it. I've been pepper sprayed when I was standing with young people who were mistreated because if you're gonna lead, you need to lead. And so when they went to the Department of Education, when they went to Treasury, taking our data, our information, our social security numbers, I went and stood in the line with those employees. So see, it's not, Karen, it's not about me. It's about those people who were fired and laid off without notice, lied to about seven months. So when they went over to CFPB, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau that makes sure that your monies and your mortgages and everything are protected since the inception, they have discovered $21 billion against people like the people in this room. So going out and protesting and marching and putting Elon Musk and Donald Trump or notice, it's what we have to do because I also have had so many people respond to me that it gave them hope that someone was outside of the office, outside of the pen and paper, standing up, making them feel that hope was on the way. And if you think about most of the things in our history, What has gotten us? Think about the Affordable Care Act. When those women, black, white, and brown went out there with those pink hats. We were in the minority, and some 70 times they tried to repeal our health care, and we were victorious. Think about the right to vote. I mean, I could walk you through history, 1963, 1964, 1965, civil rights, voting rights, the job act. All of those things happened in part. because we were doing everything. We were protesting in a peaceful way. We were standing up for our freedoms and our rights. And you had leaders standing with the people. And that's the most important thing. We can't do it alone. I can go on that house floor every day and write a piece of legislation. But if you don't respond and write back and stand up and fight for what you're right, and if we don't do it together, It's not going to do anything. And so I think it's making a small difference because we have 90-some executive orders by Donald Trump. But we've also had, in certain legal cases, in one case, 19 attorney generals from 22 states. And they were victorious. They were able to get those dollars that were frozen. They're no longer frozen because they filed cases. We had 22 attorney generals to file lawsuits. So people aren't afraid. But what happens this morning? They fire our judge in the Ohio Southern District. Judge Parker walked and fired him today. So if we don't speak up right up, educate up, legislate up, litigate up, We're gonna go back in history, and I sometimes believe history repeats itself. We will go into a dictatorship.",
