  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "We know that most of it is not legal. Now, look at this. Look at this room and you understand, you vote, you understand how we elect people. You explained to me how Elon Musk can speak to America from the Oval Office. He was not vetted. He was not elected by anyone. How can he walk in and fire people? just because the president said he's my friend and he's a billionaire? No, it's not legal. Nor is it legal for him to say only the person that he has assigned to this role can do that. It's not legal and the good thing is, because I always want to leave some hope. There are attorney generals, there are law firms, there are people who are standing up and fighting. And here's the other good news that I thank you for voting. We are only three short of members in the Congress. We only need three Republicans. We only need three Republicans. Our margin is 215 to 218. So I'm not here just blowing up smoke and saying, we only need three. And you're not going to convince me that out of 218 people that they all believe that this is OK and this is legal. And you know why? Because now it's affecting them. You see, when people talk about taking away programs, many people sit around and say, well, I don't get any money for that program. It's not going to affect me. Now they're talking about Medicare. So all you have to do is be 65 years old, has nothing to do with your income or someone who's poor. Talking about Medicare, talking about Social Security, talking about public education, dismantling that that will hurt our children and our grandchildren. So we have to continue. What we need private sector, we need attorneys who band together as individuals and take this on, we need an army of folks to join us.",
