    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "It has impact here because when you look at, when they do away with something, whether it's in healthcare, the $230 million with food assistance, when you look at child care, sure it has effect. It has effect on the number of people who will lose their jobs here. because the reach has come down into the government level at the county level. So yes, it will have effect. Prices will go up. Come on, you know, if you put tariffs and taxes, I think we already know. I'm in Washington, D.C. four out of the five working days. And I went into the grocery store just to kind of price out some things. Eggs in Washington DC, $14.99 for one dozen of regular eggs, not organic, not anything, $14.99. I went to the store here in my district. Eggs were $7.99. I remember when I could get eggs for $1.99 or $2.49. So it's having an effect. I put the eggs back. Now, I obviously had 799 and 1499, but it was a principle because the lady next to me, she said, I can't do this. I can't afford half of this stuff in here. And I felt her pain. I mean, it is affecting us, absolutely.",
