  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "I think that's the fear. Come on. If you want to be a dictator, then you're afraid of all the other people who are successful, all the other people who are in your way in your space. And what diversity, equity, and inclusion did, it opened up the world in banking, in tech, in just about every operation to look like this room. Now, you didn't say diversity, equity, and inclusion to come out here to this program. You said, let's come and learn about what we need to know in our communities, in our Congress, in our world. and DE and I, diversity, equity, and inclusion showed up. Nobody said you had to have so many of this, so many of that, but when you're doing the right thing like CMC is doing, this is what you get. So I don't want people to think diversity and equity and inclusion was a game or stage. We simply said, and I can say this, I was the first ever in the United States Congress to be the chair of diversity and inclusion. And at that time, we brought in every bank president. that made had more than $50 billion in assets in their bank. And we lined them up. And every one of them came. Pick them. Wells Fargo, Pick, US Bank, Bank of America, JP Morgan, Chase, Goldman Sachs. They, all of the presidents and CEOs came. And we asked them not a number, not a quota. I don't believe in that. And whoever came up with that idea years ago, when we started a affirmative action that 8% or 12% or 15% was good, we were crazy. But we were so thirsty to get something. Because remember, it was on a scale of 100. Come on, you can do math. Heck, it's 8% or 10%. on a 100% scale, something you should brag about. But we move the needle with them. You started getting diversity and equity managers. Then we made them no longer report to HR, but to report to the president and CEO. Then we went in and looked at who's on your board. We didn't find women, black, white, brown, or other. So then we started going after them to make sure that they were more inclusive. And we didn't stop there because they would go and say, oh, I hired 12 minority employees. And he said, well, where do they work and report to? Nothing wrong with you being on a line job, but none of them were up in the C-suites. Then we start looking at, well, who's on your board? And I can tell you within the two years with that, we move the needle. People, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, people who lived in rural America is not just race and gender. People got jobs in the C-suite. More women start being placed on corporate boards. And that was because we were intentional. It was not a social thing to do. It was a business thing to do. And it was what was good for America. And so now you have people of power who look like this room. And now if you're going to have a dictatorship, then you dismantle that. And what else do you dismantle? Universities and education that educate people. And that's what's happening and we're going to continue to fight against it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
