  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "I think that's a very good question. And so let me answer it like this. When you're given out $4 trillion in tax breaks for the top percent, and then Republicans are saying there is waste in government, and they're doing away the programs and the things that are not discretionary. They can rid of mandatory programs that was set by the government. Now, what we are doing is participating in an appropriation process, a budget process, and an oversight process to look at the waste. Now, I'm not going to say that government is perfect and we don't have things that need to be cut. So the way this works, we submit a budget, Republicans submit a budget, and a lot of the other organizations will put their thoughts to what they think the budget should look like. And in all of those budgets, people either cut, transfer, or ask for less or more money. And so we support taking waste out of government. What we don't support is blindly saying that waste is doing away with the Affordable Care Act and the insurance that people have or letting 26-year-olds still stay on their parents' insurance. We don't believe that you do away with the waste by getting ready rid of tax incentive programs and programs that help people with their utility bills. And so what they're doing is just going right across the board and saying basically, and these are my words, basically, well, if you're poor and poor, everything is relative. Everybody in this room is poor, according to what they're doing, unless you're talking about having trillions and hundreds of billions of dollars. So they have no regard for hard-working people. And so their definition of waste in our definition of waste is entirely different. Now, raising the debt ceiling, let me just tell you, we have raised this debt ceiling under Trump when he was the president in 2017. We've raised the debt ceiling under Barack Obama. We've raised the debt ceiling on Joe Biden, but with reason. Now, here's what happens, and you need to remember this in knowing how it operates to that person, and it's a good question. But here's the thing. It's when you ask for something, they ask to run this country. They have the administration, they have the Senate, and they have the House. Now, you tell me what they're running. You have two people who are running this country and making the determination on the debt ceiling, and the waste. That's not our responsibility, but we're not irresponsible, so we're trying to participate in this. But that question needs to be asked to everybody that took the oath to serve us, that sits on the other side of the aisle that are chairing these committees. And they're not answering your questions because they know there's no way in heck. that they can tell you they're doing the right thing.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
