  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the only thing we'd have to do is keep in mind, while they can dismantle and rebuild, it's going to take more than a day or a month. It's the two-year cycle. So it's voting. It's getting people registered. You know, when you think of it, you're thinking of what's happening in this country with a number of people who are in the United States. And then you're talking about 218 people stopping that. Something's wrong with the math with that. So the same questions you are asking me, they're coming back to you. What are you doing? I mean, you come, you know, people are saying, well, what are we doing? We're using the pen, we're using the voice, we're using the protest. How many people here have written a letter? I'm not going to embarrass you. How many people in here wrote a letter to speaker Johnson? How many people even know who Michael Johnson is? There's somebody in this room who does not know their Congress person. There's somebody in this room that has never written a letter and is going to lose part of their health care benefits. So the question comes back. If this comes to fruition, then we're going to need a lot more than Joyce Beatty trying to give you hope. Because you know what'll happen? Then you'll say, Congresswoman, how do we fight? Where do I go? Because see, I got this letter. And they're telling me in 10 days, I no longer have the loan on my mortgage. So I'm trying to help you not be in that position. This is not just for people who live on the street. Do you know what a homeless man, when I stopped to ask him how he was going, he said, Congresswoman? So don't confuse poverty with intellect. I get a lot of wisdom from this guy, I said, hey, what are you thinking? You know what he said to me? He said, I'm thinking that it is not going to bother me. I'm already eating out of trash cans and I'm already on the street. He said, so you better go tell the people. It's not the poor people that they're thinking about. It's the hard working Americans. It's those folks that make 100 and 200 and 300 and 400 thousands of dollars that thought they were free. He said, with this administration, if your question comes to reality, you're not gonna be any different than Mr. Henry who's out there on the street because he's not coming after them. He's coming after our parents and he's coming out of our children. Do you mean children who are sick and ill and disabled are on Medicaid? Most people say, oh, that's Medicaid, I'm not on any tan for welfare. Think about those children. who are getting Medicaid. And then think about in this room. Anybody in this room other than me, the 65 years older. They're getting ready to take y'all's Medicare,",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
