  {
    "episode_id": 77013,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77013.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "Let me say thank you and let me say one thing about this. I can tell you when we have congressional days and individuals who come with disabilities, they have one of the largest groups of coming in and draw more attention. They have been fearless. They have come into the house chambers in wheelchairs, on crutches and everything else and refused to leave. So I want to thank you and say to you the population that you're referencing, we need more of us to follow what they've done because they understand clear what they have to lose with medical. For us, well, I don't need it unless I get sick. Well, every day they may need a new wheelchair or like your father, they have to have round the care care. But here's the other thing to the first part. We will be your voice. And that's why I opened this with diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility. Because it is really unfair. If you come after me because I'm female, I'm over 40, which is something to discriminate against, and I'm a black American, that's one thing. But when you come after people with disabilities and are veterans, everybody in this room ought to be writing a letter for his father. Everybody in this room ought to be writing a letter for those who are marginalized and have a disability. Come on, this is our family, our community. You got up, got dressed up and came out here. So all I'm asking you to do is to remember his question. to remember what he posed to me, which means is to the CMC. Every organization that you work for is in jeopardy of the people in this room. And you ought to be going back to work. It's not a partisan letter. It's a survival letter. Everybody ought to be writing a letter to our two senators. Every day, every day, write it in red, write it in green, write it in black, write it in blue, and tell them why you're writing it. You're writing it in black for power. You're writing it in blue for justice. You're writing it in red because you're angry. You're writing it in green because they're messing with your economy and your economics. And that's our survival. And that's what we have to do. I do it every day, and I'm no different than you. Every day I get on a plane, or three to four times a week, I get on a plane both ways. I get up at 5.30 AM, just about every morning. And I'm picked up and I go into my office. I go on the United States House floor. I never miss a meeting in the morning for our web, for our caucus, because that's how I get the information. And then everybody like Karen and every news outlet and every microphone you give me, I stand up for the American people and we will stand up for you young man.",
